  <!--
    Glyph geometries, one key per meaning.

    These used to be inline Data="M..." literals at the call site, which made
    "what does the refresh icon look like" a search through nineteen rows and
    left two glyphs that mean the same thing free to drift apart. A view now
    references Glyph.<Name>; a new glyph gets a key here instead of a literal.

    Source: https://github.com/tabler/tabler-icons v3.50.0 (MIT), outline set,
    `icons/outline/<name>.svg`. Every entry is the upstream `d` attributes of
    that file concatenated verbatim (nothing is redrawn by hand), and the
    comment above each key names the icon it came from, so a reviewer can diff
    an entry against the library instead of eyeballing it. The set is a single
    family on purpose: the previous registry mixed Feather-style 24x24 outlines
    with hand-drawn small-viewBox marks, which is why some rows in the sidebar
    read heavier than others at the same StrokeThickness.

    Two keys are deliberately NOT from the library: Glyph.WindowMinimize and
    Glyph.WindowMaximize. They are OS window chrome, drawn on a 0..12 grid so a
    1px stroke lands on the pixel grid at every DPI; the Tabler equivalents
    (minus / square) carry a 2-unit stroke that renders chunky in a 46x32 title
    bar button. Everything that is a *feature* icon comes from the library.

    PathGeometry (not Geometry) because Geometry has no content property:
    its mini-language is what the Figures converter accepts.
  -->

